<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import StateDemo from "$ui/demos/state/StateDemo.abide"
  import StateTransformDemo from "$ui/demos/state/StateTransformDemo.abide"
  import SharedTallyDemo from "$ui/demos/state/SharedTallyDemo.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1><code>state</code> — the owned value</h1>
  <p class="lead">The first of the three primitives. A <code>state</code> is a scope-free reactive atom that keeps only what it <strong>owns</strong> — derivation is <a href="/memo"><code>memo</code></a>'s job, and pushing is <a href="/channel"><code>channel</code></a>'s. It ships from <code>abide/shared/state</code>, so it is <strong>isomorphic</strong>: the same import in a page, a component, or a plain server <code>.ts</code>. Under each live demo is the exact source that powers it — read live from disk and highlighted on the server.</p>
  <Demo id="state" title="<code>state(v)</code> — a writable cell" client="src/ui/demos/state/StateDemo.abide">
    <StateDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Declare it, write it, read it. Everything reading the cell updates — here, one text node.</p>
    <p>Under the sugar a cell is <strong>callable</strong>: <code>count()</code> reads (and subscribes the enclosing <code>memo</code>/<code>watch</code>), <code>count.set(next)</code> writes, <code>count.peek()</code> reads without subscribing. In a <code>.abide</code> <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> the compiler writes those calls, which is why these demos type the bare <code>count</code>; in a plain <code>.ts</code> you call the surface yourself — the <a href="/memo#server-state">server graph</a> shows that form.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="transform" title="<code>state(v, transform)</code> — an invariant on the cell" client="src/ui/demos/state/StateTransformDemo.abide">
    <StateTransformDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>The optional second argument runs on every write, so the cell can never hold a value the transform wouldn't produce — clamping, rounding, normalising. One place for the invariant, rather than every call site.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="shared" title="<code>state.shared(key, initial)</code> — cell shared by key" client="src/ui/demos/state/SharedTallyDemo.abide">
    <SharedTallyDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Two component instances call <code>state.shared("reactivity-tally", 0)</code> with the SAME key, so they share one backing cell — bump either and both update. The value also syncs across other open tabs (via a <code>BroadcastChannel</code>); on the server <code>.shared</code> degrades to a per-render cell.</p>
    <p>Reading a cell is how something subscribes to it. To push the other way — out of the graph, into a side effect — use <a href="/watch"><code>watch</code></a>.</p>
    <p class="note">A module-level <code>state</code> on the server is <strong>process-global</strong> — one value across every request. Right for a derived or immutable-source graph (see the <a href="/memo#server-state">server graph</a>); for mutable per-request or per-user state reach for <code>memo: {'{'} shared {'}'}</code>, which is guarded and identity-safe.</p>
  </aside>
</main>
